Netmask
In an IP address, there are network bits and host bits
Host bit represents the number
a netmask 255.255.255.0 says that first 3 parts belong to same network and last part is for the hosts
For IP 192.168.1.42 with mask 255.255.255.0, the network is 192.168.1.0
For IP 10.10.25.18 with mask 255.255.0.0 the network is 10.10.0.0

CIDR is a modern solution to the network mask
It replaces the 255.255.255.255 with an equivalent number like /32

/8 = mask 255.0.0.0. First number is the network. One /8 network spans over 16 million addresses.
/16 = mask 255.255.0.0. First two numbers are the network. 65,536 addresses.
/24 = mask 255.255.255.0. First three numbers are the network. 256 addresses.
To get the number of hosts: eg, /24: 32-24 = 8 -> 2^8=256 
254 hosts and 2 reserved addresses 1. 
In 2 reserved addresses, 1 is Network address and the other is broadcast address

Network address: The lowest address in the subnet, the one with all host bits set to zero, is the network address. It is the name of the subnet itself, not a machine. For 192.168.1.0/24 that is 192.168.1.0
It appears in routing tables and firewall rules to mean "this whole subnet", so no host is allowed to use it.

Broadcast address: The highest address, with all host bits set to one, is the broadcast address. For 192.168.1.0/24 that is 192.168.1.255. A packet sent to it is delivered to every machine on the subnet at once. 
That is what broadcast means: one send, all receive. Machines use it for questions where the sender does not yet know who should answer, like "who on this network owns this address?". 

You will meet those messages in a later topic.
Same network or not
Destination 192.168.1.77: network part 192.168.1. Same. Local, deliver directly.
Destination 192.168.2.77: network part 192.168.2. Different. Remote, send to the gateway.
Destination 8.8.8.8: network part 8.8.8. Different. Remote, send to the gateway.
